<h2>The transaction that never was</h2>
<p>The FIR — No. 09/2025 dated 5 June 2025, registered at PS Special Cell (IFSO) — records a fraud of brutal simplicity. On 4 June 2025 the complainant was induced to transfer Rs 1 crore by RTGS from his Axis Bank account to a Bank of Baroda account held in the name of a political outfit, on the assurance that he would receive Rs 1.20 crore in cash in return. What he actually received was a red suitcase of counterfeit currency — novelty notes of the kind sold as toys — topped up with books for weight. Approximately Rs 92,56,500 of the transferred amount remains unrecovered.</p>
<p>The applicant moved the High Court under Section 482 of the BNSS (the provision corresponding to Section 438 CrPC) after apprehending arrest in the case.</p>

<h2>The rival cases</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <strong>For the applicant.</strong> He was not named in the complaint or the FIR; the implication rested mainly on a co-accused&rsquo;s disclosure statement, which is weak material; every other accused in the case was already on bail, so parity applied; he was willing to join the investigation; there was no risk of flight or tampering.
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <strong>For the State.</strong> The applicant was the principal conspirator and the ultimate beneficiary of the money; he had skipped a notice under Section 35(3) BNSS requiring his appearance; custodial interrogation was necessary to trace the unrecovered Rs 92.56 lakh, establish the modus operandi and identify the rest of the syndicate.
  </div>
</div>

<h2>What the court held</h2>
<p>Justice Vimal Kumar Yadav dismissed the application. The order opens with an observation aimed well beyond the individual case: &ldquo;Internet wolves, operating in packs, are increasingly on the prowl in cyberspace, acting in tandem and exploiting technology&rdquo; — a description of the organised, role-divided character of modern fraud syndicates, in which each member performs a discrete function and the principal stays furthest from the paper trail.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>More than a disclosure statement.</strong> The court found that material beyond the co-accused&rsquo;s disclosure — electronic evidence and the money trail — prima facie connected the applicant to the offence. The evidentiary worth of that material is for the trial; at the bail stage it was enough that the connection was not built on the disclosure alone.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Parity rejected.</strong> The applicant&rsquo;s role was distinct from, and graver than, that of the co-accused who were on bail. An alleged principal conspirator cannot ride on the bail orders of subordinate players.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Evasion weighed heavily.</strong> The applicant had not honoured a Section 35(3) BNSS notice for his appearance. The court held that &ldquo;his subsequent willingness to cooperate, by itself, does not adequately address the apprehension&rdquo; arising from that conduct.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Custodial interrogation necessary.</strong> With over Rs 92 lakh untraced and a syndicate to map, &ldquo;the requirement of custodial interrogation, in the facts of the present case, cannot be brushed aside.&rdquo; The application was accordingly dismissed.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the order matters</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Parity has limits.</strong> The order is a clean illustration of the settled rule that parity turns on comparability of roles, not on the bare fact that co-accused are out on bail.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 35(3) notices are not optional.</strong> Skipping an appearance notice is increasingly fatal in anticipatory bail practice. The notice regime presupposes cooperation; evasion converts a borderline case into a refusal.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Money trails drive outcomes.</strong> In economic offences, the fraction of the proceeds recovered is often the single most important fact. A large unrecovered balance supplies the investigative justification for custody that defeats pre-arrest bail.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Other cases count.</strong> The court also noted that the applicant faced another case with a similar modus operandi — antecedents of the same pattern reinforce the inference of an organised operation.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">The order does not decide guilt. It decides only that, at this stage, the investigation&rsquo;s need to interrogate the applicant in custody outweighs his plea for pre-arrest protection. The observations on the merits are prima facie and will not bind the trial court. For accused persons, the practical lesson sits earlier in the timeline: respond to Section 35(3) BNSS notices, document cooperation, and raise parity only where the roles genuinely align.</div>
